Title: The Tavern
Order: 4
Production Code: 1x04

Radio play, Part 4 of 6. Adapts Part 3 of the novella. Running time about 20 minutes. CONTENT NOTE: period racial slurs are spoken by Wilkes and the tavern company, as in the source; they are kept verbatim.

EXT. THE RICHMOND ROAD - ABOUT SUNSET - 1841

AMB: a country road in late light, crickets starting, a far-off cart

MUSIC: BED IN - low strings, uneasy, the string note now bent a little flat

SFX: a horse walking slowly, tired, a rider's weight in the saddle

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Just upon the edge of the great road from Petersburg, Virginia, to Richmond, and only about fifteen miles from the latter place, there stands a somewhat ancient and famous public tavern, quite notorious in its better days, as being the grand resort for most of the leading gamblers, horse-racers, cock-fighters, and slave-traders from all the country round about.

MUSIC: BED OUT

AMB: the tavern, from outside: a loose shutter, a rotten board, bats in the eaves

SFX: three heavy dogs stirring on a plank floor, one growl

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
This old rookery, the nucleus of all sorts of birds, mostly those of ill omen, has, like everything else peculiar to Virginia, lost much of its ancient consequence and splendor. The platform of the portico is a rickety affair; its planks are loose, and in some places entirely gone, leaving effective mantraps in their stead for nocturnal ramblers.

SFX: a boot going through a plank, a man's curse, a laugh

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
It was at this old tavern, while on a second visit to the State of Virginia in 1841, that Mr. Listwell, unacquainted with the fame of the place, turned aside, about sunset, to pass the night.

EXT. THE RICHMOND ROAD TAVERN - PORTICO - DUSK

AMB: dusk, dogs, men's voices on a porch, a fiddle somewhere inside

SFX: a horse stopping, a rider dismounting, a bridle clinking

Wilkes is on him in a heartbeat.

WILKES
(bland, accommodating, too friendly)
Fine evening, sir.

LISTWELL
(wary)
Very fine. This is a tavern, I believe?

WILKES
O yes, sir, yes; although you may think it looks a little the worse for wear, it was once as good a house as any in Virginy. I make no doubt if ye spend the night here, you'll think it a good house yet; for there aint a more accommodating man in the country than you'll find the landlord.

LISTWELL
The most I want is a good bed for myself, and a full manger for my horse. If I get these, I shall be quite satisfied.

WILKES
Well, I alloys like to hear a gentleman talk for his horse; and just becase the horse can't talk for itself. Now, sir, I likes a horse, and I'll guarantee your horse will be taken good care on here. That old stable, for all you see it looks so shabby now, once sheltered the great Eclipse, when he run here agin Batchelor and Jumping Jemmy.

LISTWELL
(dry)
Indeed.

WILKES
(sidling, a sharper tone underneath)
Well, I rather reckon you've travelled a right smart distance to-day, from the look of your horse?

LISTWELL
Forty miles only.

WILKES
Well! I'll be darned if that aint a pretty good only. You're somewhat in a hurry, then, I make no doubt? I reckon I could guess if I would what you're going to Richmond for. It would'nt be much of a guess either; for it's rumored hereabouts that there's to be the greatest sale of niggers at Richmond to-morrow that has taken place there in a long time; and I'll be bound you're a going there to have a hand in it.

SFX: Listwell's breath, caught, held, released

LISTWELL
(carefully neutral)
Why, you must think, then, that there's money to be made at that business?

WILKES
(a wheedle)
Well, 'pon my honor, sir, I never made any that way myself; but it stands to reason that it's a money making business; for almost all other business in Virginia is dropped to engage in this. One thing is sartain, I never see'd a nigger-buyer yet that had'nt a plenty of money, and he was'nt as free with it as water.

SFX: a coin pressed into a palm

LISTWELL
(quietly)
What may I call your name, sir?

WILKES
Well, now, they calls me Wilkes. I'm known all around by the gentlemen that comes here. They all knows old Wilkes.

LISTWELL
Well, Wilkes, you seem to be acquainted here, and I see you have a strong liking for a horse. Be so good as to speak a kind word for mine to the hostler to-night, and you'll not lose anything by it.

WILKES
(delighted, pleased with himself)
Well, sir, I see you don't say much, but I've got an insight into things. It's alloys wise to get the good will of them that's acquainted about a tavern; for a man don't know when he goes into a house what may happen, or how much he may need a friend.

SFX: a gloating grin, in the sound of Wilkes's breath

LISTWELL
(tired of him)
Thank you. I will take my supper in my room.

INT. THE RICHMOND ROAD TAVERN - BAR-ROOM - NIGHT

AMB: a crowded bar, glasses, laughter, a fiddle badly played, a fire

SFX: a bottle set down, men calling for whiskey

The Bar-Room Fraternity are in good spirits.

WILKES
(complaining, disappointed)
Whar's that gentleman?

THE LANDLORD
(off, bored)
He has gone to his room, and has ordered his supper to be brought to him.

A LOAFER
(shouting across)
Who is he, Wilkes? Where's he going?

WILKES
Well, now, I'll be hanged if I know; but I'm willing to make any man a bet of this old hat agin a five dollar bill that that gent is as full of money as a dog is of fleas. He's going down to Richmond to buy niggers, I make no doubt. He's no fool, I warrant ye.

A SECOND LOAFER
Well, he acts d----d strange, anyhow. I likes to see a man, when he comes up to a tavern, to come straight into the bar-room, and show that he's a man among men. Nobody was going to bite him.

SFX: a cup slapped on a table

WILKES
Now, I don't blame him a bit for not coming in here. That man knows his business, and means to take care on his money.

A THIRD LOAFER
Wilkes, you're a fool. You only say that, becase you hope to get a few coppers out on him.

WILKES
(wounded)
You only measure my corn by your half-bushel.

A SECOND LOAFER
(sneering)
O Wilkes! You're known here. You'll praise up anybody that will give you a copper; besides, 'tis my opinion that that fellow who took his long slab-sides up stairs, for all the world just like a half-scared woman, afraid to look honest men in the face, is a Northerner, and as mean as dish-water.

WILKES
Now what will you bet of that?

SFX: a laugh, a crash of dice, a quarrel starting, a chair overturned

MUSIC: BED IN - low strings, a slow dread under the noise

INT. THE RICHMOND ROAD TAVERN - LISTWELL'S ROOM - NIGHT

AMB: the same room, but heard through floorboards: muffled, gappy, laughter rising and falling

SFX: boots on boards overhead, a man lying down, a bed creaking

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He ordered his supper to be brought to him in his private room, private to the eye, but not to the ear. This room was directly over the bar, and the plastering being off, nothing but pine boards and naked laths separated him from the disagreeable company below.

SFX: below, a story told with relish, men roaring, a duel recounted, a gun mimed with a bang of a fist

SFX: below, a crash, a shout, a scuffle, a fiddle abruptly stopped

LISTWELL
(under his breath, appalled)
Good God.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It would not be proper here to give the reader any idea of the vulgarity and dark profanity which rolled, as "a sweet morsel," under these corrupt tongues. A more brutal set of creatures, perhaps, never congregated.

SFX: Listwell turning in a creaking bed, restless

MUSIC: BED OUT

AMB: the bar, quieter, a last whiskey poured, the fiddle starting again slowly

SFX: a clock striking eleven, slow and uneven

MUSIC: STING - low strings, a sudden hard chord

AMB: the road outside, noise growing, wagons and cracked whips and many feet

SFX: dozens of people crowding into the house, doors, boots, the rasp of chains on stone steps

SFX: whips cracking, the clank of chains, a hundred and more at once

SFX: loud laughing, loud singing, loud cursing, and somewhere under it all, weeping

MUSIC: SOURCE BED IN - a plantation song, sung by many voices, low and cracked, under the noise

LISTWELL
(whispered, to himself, horrified)
They've brought them in. Dear God. They've brought them in.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was loud laughing, loud singing, loud cursing, and yet there seemed to be weeping and mourning in the midst of all.

SFX: Listwell sitting up in bed, his breathing short

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
He had heard enough during the forepart of the night to convince him that a buyer of men and women stood the best chance of being respected. And he, therefore, thought it best to say nothing which might undo the favorable opinion that had been formed of him in the bar-room.

LISTWELL
(bitter, ashamed, almost silent)
Having as little of the spirit of a martyr as Erasmus, I think it wiser to trust the mercy of God for my soul, than the humanity of slave-traders for my body.

SFX: the sound of chains below, endless, rolling

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Bodily fear, not conscientious scruples, prevailed.

MUSIC: SOURCE BED OUT

AMB: the house settling, the chains going quiet one by one, a last dog's growl, the dawn

EXT. THE RICHMOND ROAD TAVERN - BOWLING ALLEY - DAWN

AMB: early morning, a cold yard, a rooster, a heavy silence in the old building

SFX: a barn door pushed with an effort on rotten hinges

NARRATOR (V.O.)
On reaching the alley Mr. Listwell saw, for the first time in his life, a slave-gang on their way to market. A sad sight truly. Here were one hundred and thirty human beings, children of a common Creator, guilty of no crime, men and women, with hearts, minds, and deathless spirits, chained and fettered, and bound for the market, in a christian country, in a country boasting of its liberty, independence, and high civilization.

SFX: chains shifting as a hundred and thirty people stir at once, a long iron whisper

SFX: a child coughing, a woman murmuring a name, a man clearing his throat

The Slave Gang has sat up in the dark to face the light.

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Humanity converted into merchandise, and linked in iron bands.

SFX: Listwell's footsteps, going slowly along the line, stopping

SFX: a man's breath, a man's chains, a man looking up

Madison Washington looks at him and holds the look. Then, after a moment, his face opens.

LISTWELL
(a shocked gasp, then a whisper)
Madison. Is that you?

MADISON
(cheerful, almost easy, for the others' sake)
O yes, sir. They've got me again.

SFX: Listwell crossing the last steps, a hand falling on Madison's shoulder

LISTWELL
(barely audible)
How? How is this possible?

SFX: Madison's chains, a small shake of the head, a warning

MADISON
(low, urgent)
They will soon be out to look after us. You can come when they go to breakfast, and I will tell you all.

SFX: Listwell stepping back, out of the door, the bar of light closing

SFX: the man next to Madison, chained to him, his voice low and curious

THE MAN IN CHAINS
Who is that gentleman?

MADISON
(quietly to him, with authority)
He is a friend of mine. I cannot tell you now. Suffice it to say he is a friend. You shall hear more of him before long; but mark me. Whatever shall pass between that gentleman and me, in your hearing, I pray you will say nothing about it. We are all chained here together. Ours is a common lot; and that gentleman is not less your friend than mine.

AMB: the whole gang, breathing, a hush that is almost a sigh of hope

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It seems that Madison, by that mesmeric power which is the invariable accompaniment of genius, had already won the confidence of the gang, and was a sort of general-in-chief among them.

SFX: the door dragged open, three men's boots, spurs, a pistol butt knocked on a post

The Keepers enter as they have been entering for years.

THE FIRST KEEPER
Hallo! Hallo! Are you all there?

MADISON
All here.

THE SECOND KEEPER
(rough, pleased)
Well, well, that's right. Your journey will soon be over. You'll be in Richmond by eleven to-day, and then you'll have an easy time on it.

SFX: a girl's crying, soft, stubborn, in the middle of the gang

THE THIRD KEEPER
(sharp)
I say, gal, what in the devil are you crying about? I'll give you something to cry about, if you don't mind.

SFX: a whip drawn off a peg, the girl's crying stopping at once as if cut

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This was said to a girl apparently not more than twelve years old, who had been weeping bitterly. She had, probably, left behind her a loving mother, affectionate sisters, brothers, and friends. The dealers in human flesh have no respect for such sorrow. They look upon it as a protest against their cruel injustice, and they are prompt to punish it.

SFX: a bell ringing across the yard, a long bright clanging

THE FIRST KEEPER
(to the others)
Breakfast!

SFX: three men's boots hurrying off at once, fighting for the door

AMB: the gang, breathing, the yard growing quiet

SFX: Listwell running back across the yard, breath short, gravel under his boots

LISTWELL
(whisper, desperate)
Now do tell me all about the matter. Do you know me?

MADISON
(quick, steady)
Oh, yes. I know you well, and shall never forget you nor that cold and dreary night you gave me shelter. I must be short, for they'll soon be out again.

MUSIC: BED IN - low cello, slow, rising

MADISON (CONT'D)
On reaching Canada, and getting over the excitement of making my escape, my thoughts turned to my poor wife, who had well deserved my love by her virtuous fidelity and undying affection for me. I could not bear the thought of leaving her in the cruel jaws of slavery, without making an effort to rescue her.

SFX: a woman's voice remembered, far off, calling a name

Susan speaks here as Madison hears her in dreams. She is not in the room.

SUSAN
(distant, an echo)
O Madison. Madison. Will you then leave me here? Can you leave me here to die? No. No. You will come. You will come.

MADISON (CONT'D)
(his voice tight)
I was wretched. I lost my appetite. I could neither work, eat, nor sleep, till I resolved to hazard my own liberty, to gain that of my wife.

EXT. THE PLANTATION - NIGHT - MADISON'S MEMORY

AMB: night, a plantation, crickets, the wind in a chinaberry tree, a far-off dog

SFX: a ladder set against a wall, wood on stone

SFX: a window raised, a rasp of frame on sash

MADISON (V.O.)
Six weeks ago I reached my old master's place. I laid about the neighborhood nearly a week, watching my chance, and, finally, I ventured upon the desperate attempt to reach my poor wife's room by means of a ladder.

SFX: a woman's scream, sudden, high, then the dull thud of a body falling

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I reached the window, but the noise in raising it frightened my wife, and she screamed and fainted.

SFX: Madison, quiet, lifting her, breathing, descending the ladder

SFX: dogs, all at once, barking, a pack, a house waking

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I took her in my arms, and was descending the ladder, when the dogs began to bark furiously, and before I could get to the woods the white folks were roused.

SFX: a door thrown open, men's voices, boots, a rifle lock

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
The cool night air soon restored my wife, and she readily recognized her husband. We made the best of our way to the woods, but it was now too late. The dogs were after us as though they would have torn us to pieces.

SFX: two people running, breathing, branches whipping past, dogs closing

Madison's Master and his two sons run out with loaded rifles.

THE MASTER
(off, hoarse with rage)
Stop. Stop, or be shot down.

SFX: running, no answer, and in the running the unmistakable sound of two people hand in hand

SFX: a rifle cracks, then another, in the dark

MUSIC: STING - one terrible low string, held

SFX: a woman's footsteps stopping, a body falling beside a man, the soft impact of earth

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(quietly, to the end)
My poor wife fell by my side dead, while I received but a slight flesh wound.

AMB: the wood, the dogs closing, nothing else

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I now became desperate, and stood my ground, and awaited their attack over her dead body. They rushed upon me with their rifles in hand. I parried their blows, and fought them till I was knocked down and overpowered.

SFX: blows, a cry, a man's body falling, chains being thrown on and locked

MUSIC: BED OUT

INT. THE RICHMOND ROAD TAVERN - BOWLING ALLEY - DAWN

AMB: the gang, quiet, a rooster, the yard

SFX: Listwell's hand on the wall, unsteady

LISTWELL
(very low)
It was madness to have returned.

MADISON
(not angry, not even hurt)
Sir, I could not be free with the galling thought that my poor wife was still a slave. With her in slavery, my body, not my spirit, was free.

SFX: the chains on his ankles, a small clink as he shifts

MADISON (CONT'D)
I was taken to the house, chained to a ring-bolt, my wounds dressed. I was kept there three days. All the slaves for miles around were brought to see me. Many slave-holders came with their slaves, using me as proof of the completeness of their power, and of the impossibility of slaves getting away. I was taunted, jeered at, and berated by them in a manner that pierced me to the soul. Thank God, I was able to smother my rage, and to bear it all with seeming composure.

SFX: a long slow exhale, the sound of rage held in a closed chest

MADISON (CONT'D)
After my wounds were nearly healed, I was taken to a tree and stripped, and I received sixty lashes on my naked back. A few days after, I was sold to a slave-trader, and placed in this gang for the New Orleans market.

LISTWELL
(quick, hopeful)
Do you think your master would sell you to me?

MADISON
(gently, no hope in it)
O no, sir. I was sold on condition of my being taken South. Their motive is revenge.

SFX: Listwell's hands closed on the rail, a long silence, the chains softly moving

LISTWELL
(hoarse)
Then I fear I can do nothing for you. Put your trust in God, and bear your sad lot with the manly fortitude which becomes a man. I shall see you at Richmond, but don't recognize me.

SFX: coins pressed into a palm, ten of them, silver

LISTWELL (CONT'D)
(quick)
Take this. Ten dollars.

SFX: the gang, a ripple of low voices, a dozen "God bless you"s whispered at once

SFX: Listwell's quick footsteps going

INT. THE RICHMOND ROAD TAVERN - DINING ROOM - MORNING

AMB: a breakfast table, plates, forks, men talking with their mouths full, a sunlit room

SFX: a cup of coffee set down, a spoon barely touched, a man staring into it

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Fearful of exciting suspicion by too long delay, our friend went to the breakfast table, with the air of one who half reproved the greediness of those who rushed in at the sound of the bell. A cup of coffee was all that he could manage.

MUSIC: BED IN - low strings, uncomfortable

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
The strangeness of meeting again one whom he had met on two several occasions before, under extraordinary circumstances, was well calculated to suggest the idea that a supernatural power, a wakeful providence, or an inexorable fate, had linked their destiny together.

SFX: chairs pushed back, men leaving, a plate scraped

INT. THE RICHMOND ROAD TAVERN - BAR-ROOM - MORNING

AMB: the bar-room by day, flies, a broom sweeping, whiskey already poured

SFX: Listwell's boots on boards, firm, resolute

Wilkes is at his elbow at once.

WILKES
(cheerful, intimate)
Them's a likely set of niggers in the alley there.

LISTWELL
(hard, flat, performing)
Yes, they're fine looking fellows. One of them I should like to purchase, and for him I would be willing to give a handsome sum.

SFX: Wilkes turning to a friend, a triumphant chuckle

WILKES
Aha, Bill, did you hear that? I told you I know'd that gentleman wanted to buy niggers, and would bid as high as any purchaser in the market.

LISTWELL
Come, come. Don't be too loud in your praise. You are old enough to know that prices rise when purchasers are plenty.

WILKES
That's a fact. I see you knows the ropes. There's not a man in old Virginy whom I'd rather help to make a good bargain than you, sir.

SFX: a silver dollar thrown, a hand catching it in the air

LISTWELL
(tight)
Take that for your kind good will.

SFX: Wilkes holding the dollar to the light, a grin of victory

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Mr. Listwell now stood as well with the company as any other occupant of the bar-room.

MUSIC: BED OUT

EXT. THE RICHMOND ROAD - THE COFFLE - MORNING

AMB: a long road, dust, the sun, no wind, a hundred feet in chains

SFX: a hundred and thirty pairs of feet on a hard road, chain links dragging, a whip that falls and falls

MUSIC: SOURCE BED IN - a hand drum, steady, a march, slow and heavy, with a low humming over it

NARRATOR (V.O.)
We pass over the hurry and bustle, the brutal vociferations of the slave-drivers in getting their unhappy gang in motion for Richmond; and we need not narrate every application of the lash to those who faltered in the journey.

SFX: a horse following far behind, a rider's tired breathing

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Mr. Listwell followed the train at a long distance, with a sad heart.

MUSIC: SOURCE BED OUT

EXT. RICHMOND - THE WHARF - NOON

AMB: a busy wharf, gulls, hawsers, casks rolled, hawkers, a ship's bell, a crowd

SFX: a hardware store door, a bell on a string, a clerk's voice

LISTWELL
(buying, quick)
Three of your strong files, please. The strongest you have.

SFX: three files laid on a counter, tied with string, coins counted

SFX: Listwell's quick steps, pushing through the crowd, breathing hard

SFX: a small boat's oars, creaking, bumping a quay

SFX: the coffle being counted off the stones into a boat, in parcels, chains, a driver's shout

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The thought struck him that, while mixing with the multitude, he might do his friend Madison one last service.

SFX: one man passing close, the clink of chain, a rustle of a hand passing a rough bundle

The Three Files change hands in the space of a breath, and no one sees it but the listener.

SFX: the pocket of a coat, a small weight dropping in, a button, a nod

SFX: the crowd closing over the place where Listwell stood

NARRATOR (V.O.)
All the company now on board, the imperious voice of the captain sounded, and instantly a dozen hardy seamen were in the rigging, hurrying aloft to unfurl the broad canvas of our Baltimore built American Slaver.

CAPTAIN CLARKE
(off, a long way, a huge voice)
Aloft! Aloft! Make sail.

MUSIC: BED IN - the full ensemble for the first time, rising, a sea-song with a drum

AMB: a ship getting under way: halyards, blocks, canvas cracking, a windlass turning

SFX: a heavy windlass, clank, clank, the fall-bit, an anchor weighed

SFX: sails filling, a long low groan of timber, the hull beginning to move

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The sailors hung about the ropes, like so many black cats, now in the round-tops, now in the cross-trees, now on the yard-arms; all was bluster and activity. The anchors weighed, jibs, mainsails, and topsails hauled to the wind, and the long, low, black slaver, with her cargo of human flesh, careened and moved forward to the sea.

SFX: the brig leaning to a wind, water at the bow, gulls wheeling

The Creole is under way, and in her hold, in the half-dark, a man with a file in his coat does not make a sound.

EXT. RICHMOND - THE WHARF - LATER

AMB: the wharf thinning out, the water slapping, the breeze

SFX: Listwell's slow footsteps to the edge of a quay, a man stopping

MUSIC: BED OUT - the sea-song's last chord dissolving into wind

LISTWELL
(quiet, at the water, to no one)
Farewell, farewell. Brave and true man. God grant that brighter skies may smile upon your future than have yet looked down upon your thorny pathway.

AMB: the wind, the sea, the distance

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Mr. Listwell stood on the shore, and watched the slaver till the last speck of her upper sails faded from sight, and announced the limit of human vision.

SFX: Listwell's boots turning on the quay, going

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Saying this to himself, our friend lost no time in completing his business, and in making his way homewards, gladly shaking off from his feet the dust of Old Virginia.

AMB: the wharf, then the sea, an open sea, a long gentle swell

MUSIC: STING - the string note, sharp, then swallowed by wind

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Part Five. The Marine Coffee-house.

AMB: sea wind, fading to silence
